The attached image is a Western blot (Fig. 7D in the PDF) illustrating the effect of Nano-11 on IL-1β processing and secretion in LPS-primed dendritic cells. It shows that while LPS stimulation alone induces pro-IL-1β (~30kD) accumulation in cell lysates and a small amount of active IL-1β (~17kD) in the supernatant, Nano-11, particularly when combined with LPS priming, leads to significantly increased secretion of the active 17kD form of IL-1β into the supernatant. This confirms Nano-11's ability to activate the inflammasome pathway in dendritic cells, leading to the cleavage and release of the pro-inflammatory cytokine IL-1β.

The image significantly contributes to vaccine development by elucidating Nano-11's mechanism of action as an adjuvant, specifically its capacity to induce active IL-1β secretion. This inflammasome-mediated activation is crucial for driving robust innate and adaptive immune responses, including T cell activation and antibody production. Understanding this mechanism allows for the rational design of new vaccines utilizing Nano-11 to enhance immunogenicity and overcome limitations of current adjuvants, leading to more effective and protective prophylactic vaccines.